---
import floor from '@busy-office/ui/floor.json';
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import pkg from '@busy-office/ui/package.json';
import { NPM, OTHER_PACKAGE_MANAGERS } from '../../data/package-managers.mjs';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const aLaCarte = `# ORDER MATTERS: tokens, then reset, then components
import "@busy-office/ui/css/tokens";
import "@busy-office/ui/css/reset";
import "@busy-office/ui/css/components/data-table";
# a .min variant exists for every file (…/data-table.min)`;
const jsInit = `import { initDialogs, initDataTables, initTabs, initDropdowns, initAlerts }
  from "@busy-office/ui/js";

// Call each ONCE, ever — document-level delegation survives all DOM swaps.
initDialogs(); initDataTables(); initTabs(); initDropdowns(); initAlerts();`;
const checkScript = `// package.json — runs on the BUILT output, so it goes after your build
"scripts": {
  "build": "…",
  "check:markup": "bo-check-markup dist"
}

npm run check:markup        # or: npx bo-check-markup dist`;
const checkCi = `# .github/workflows/ci.yml
- run: npm ci
- run: npm run build          # must come first — the check reads rendered HTML
- run: npm run check:markup`;
const skeleton = `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-density="comfortable"> <!-- or "compact" for back-office -->
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <!-- NEVER add user-scalable=no — zoom is the WCAG 1.4.4 mechanism -->
  <!-- CSS-only pages are LIGHT, coherently — native scrollbars and form
       chrome included. Offering dark mode = setting data-theme, blocking,
       before first paint (localStorage, a cookie, or server-rendered): -->
  <script>
    document.documentElement.dataset.theme =
      localStorage.getItem("theme") ?? "light";
  </script>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/vendor/busy-office-ui@${pkg.version}/index.min.css">
  <!-- Works in ANY server stack (Rails, Django, PHP, Go): copy dist/ into
       your static/asset pipeline — no bundler, no Node at runtime. Put the
       VERSION in the vendored path: upgrades become an explicit new copy +
       one href change, and you can always tell which version is deployed. -->
</head>
<body>
  <div class="bo-app-shell">
    <header class="bo-navbar bo-app-shell__header">…</header>
    <nav class="bo-sidebar-nav bo-app-shell__sidebar" aria-label="Main">…</nav>
    <main class="bo-app-shell__main">…your screen…</main>
  </div>
  <script type="module">
    import { initDialogs, initDataTables } from "/vendor/busy-office-ui/js/index.js";
    initDialogs(); initDataTables();
  </script>
</body>
</html>`;
---
<Gallery title="Installation" description="Install busy-office-ui and import its CSS. These steps are executed on every CI run, not just written, against the package about to be published.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>These steps are executed, not just written.</strong> On every CI run
    <code>check:quickstart</code> creates an empty directory, installs the
    package that is about to be published, resolves each import named below,
    pastes a real screen from the
    <a href={base + '/getting-started/screen-kit'}>screen kit</a>, and asserts in
    a browser that the CSS actually applied — a page with no stylesheet still has
    every row and every class, so it measures computed style rather than markup.
    If anything here stops working, that build fails.
  </p>
  <p class="demo-note">Install the package and import one stylesheet — that's it. No
  build step: the output is plain CSS and standard ES modules. Two ideas show up
  immediately below and are worth a minute each: <a href={base + '/concepts/tokens'}>design
  tokens</a> (what you re-skin) and <a href={base + '/concepts/density'}>density</a>
  (the one attribute that resizes a whole screen).</p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <pre><code>{NPM.command}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Or, with the same result: {OTHER_PACKAGE_MANAGERS.map((pm, i) => (<>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}<code>{pm.command}</code></>))}</p>
    <pre><code>import "@busy-office/ui/css";   /* everything, one line */</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>No package manager?</strong> Copy
    <code>dist/css/index.min.css</code> and <code>dist/js/</code> from the
    <a href="https://github.com/Busy-Office/busy-office-ui">repo</a> (or
    <code>npm pack @busy-office/ui</code>) into your assets and link them as plain
    files — see the skeleton below.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Import only what you use <span class="bo-u-text-muted">(optional)</span></h2>
    <pre><code>{aLaCarte}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Turn on interactive components</h2>
    <pre><code>{jsInit}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Components whose page says "JS required" are inert until
    you call their init. What each does →
    <a href={base + '/concepts/js-behaviors'}>JS behaviors</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Copy-paste page skeleton</h2>
    <pre><code>{skeleton}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2 id="check-your-markup-in-ci">Check your markup in CI <span class="bo-u-text-muted">(recommended)</span></h2>
    <p>An unknown class or attribute value fails <em>silently</em> here — modern CSS
    degrades by doing nothing. One command catches both, against the same
    <code>api.json</code> the docs are generated from:</p>
    <pre><code>{checkScript}</code></pre>
    <p><strong>Point it at built HTML, not templates.</strong> It reads rendered output,
    so it must run <em>after</em> your build. If your stack renders server-side
    (Rails, Django, Laravel), point it at whatever HTML you already produce — a
    static export, or the fixtures your view tests render.</p>
    <pre><code>{checkCi}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>What it does not do</strong>, stated plainly so it isn't mistaken for more
      than it is: it checks that every <code>bo-*</code> class and every framework
      <code>data-*</code> value <em>exists</em>. It does not check that your markup
      <em>means</em> anything — a real class on the wrong element, a heading level that
      skips, a table used for layout all pass. Your own <code>data-*</code> hooks are
      never flagged, because nothing distinguishes <code>data-row-id</code> from a
      misspelled framework attribute. Use it as a spell-checker, not a reviewer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2 id="browser-floor">Browser floor</h2>
    <p><strong>{floor.label}</strong> — <strong>{floor.reach}% of tracked browser
    usage</strong> ({floor.reachSource}). The framework uses <code>@layer</code>,
    <code>:has()</code>, named container queries, and <code>popover</code>. Server-set
    <code>aria-invalid</code> error messages degrade gracefully below the floor;
    interactive <code>:has()</code> reveals do not.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>The ceiling is {floor.ceiling}%</strong>, because <code>@layer</code> is this
      framework's API — no version of it can reach further than <code>@layer</code> does.
      So the whole distance between what we ship and the best any framework of this shape
      could do is <strong>{(floor.ceiling - floor.reach).toFixed(2)} points</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      Of that, <strong>0.91 points</strong> is bought by giving up two accessibility
      features: the empty <code>alt</code> on decorative glyphs
      (<code>content: "↕" / ""</code>, which stops a screen reader announcing an arrow)
      and <code>:user-invalid</code> validation styling. We keep both. That trade is
      recorded rather than assumed — see roadmap 38.2.
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">One caveat: it assumes an unmodified root font-size. A
    host page that rescales <code>html</code> (legacy <code>62.5%</code> hacks) rescales
    every rem — embed via iframe instead.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>RF scanners and other older handheld devices</h2>
    <p>The floor above is <strong>{floor.label}</strong> — evergreen-desktop
    territory. A warehouse RF scanner or rugged handheld (Zebra, Honeywell,
    Datalogic) is a different question: its "Enterprise Browser" wrapper
    renders through the device's Android System WebView, so the WebView
    version is the real floor, and a fleet bought in 2018-2020 is not on
    evergreen Chrome by default.</p>
    <p><strong>Researched target: Chrome/WebView 108</strong> — stated at
    <strong>medium confidence</strong>, not as a guarantee. Any device that has
    taken even one vendor patch (Zebra LifeGuard, Honeywell's guaranteed
    upgrade path, Datalogic Shield) in the last ~3.5 years clears it; a fleet
    with Play blocked and no patch pipeline sits at the factory image (WebView
    61-83 depending on the shipped Android version) and is out of scope for
    any framework built on modern CSS, not a floor this project chases.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">This framework's current CSS floor
    ({floor.label}) does not reach 108 — <code>:has()</code>,
    <code>@container</code> and the logical-property shorthands used
    throughout sit above it. A lower-floor build profile for the RF-relevant
    component set is tracked as roadmap 59.2-59.3, not shipped yet. See
    <a href={base + '/patterns/goods-receipt'}>goods receipt</a> for the
    scanning interaction itself — <code>initScanInput()</code> and the
    quantity stepper are already Chrome-80-safe today and need no profile.</p>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/getting-started/first-screen", "Your first screen"], ["/concepts/cascade", "The cascade contract"], ["/getting-started/troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"]]} />
</Gallery>
